INT. NORTHERN LIGHTS CURLING CLUB - SHEET THREE - NIGHT

Pebbled ice, white and hard under flat lights. Red and yellow rings painted beneath the surface. A scoreboard ticks. The crowd, forty fans in parkas, hushes.

SKIP BRITTA LINDGREN-KOWALSKI, 40s, a wool toque and a steady squint, crouches in the house with her broom held like a rudder. At the hack, MURDOCH FINLAY, 50s, her veteran lead, gathers a stone. At the sideline, YUKI TANAKA-REID, 20s, the youngest on the team, grips a broom and bites her lip. TERRENCE ODUMODU, 30s, the vice-skip, in a quilted vest, watches the scoreboard like a hawk.

BRITTA
Hurry hard!

Murdoch releases. The stone glides. The broom crew sprints alongside it.

YUKI
Hard! Hard!

TERRENCE
Hard!

BRITTA ^
HARD!

The stone slides, hovers at the edge of the button. Nothing moves. Forty parkas hold their breath.

Then it curls. A gentle arc. A sliver of daylight. It nudges the opponent's stone aside and settles dead center.

The crowd explodes. Yuki jumps and flings her broom in the air. Terrence hugs Murdoch so hard he lifts him. Britta stays crouched, forehead on her broom handle, shaking.

A woman in an official's jacket strides across the ice, clipboard in hand.

OLUWASEUN ADEBAYO, 50s, the head official, wears a tight bun and a sour look. She inspects the stone with a tape measure.

OLUWASEUN
Measure's confirmed. Team Lindgren takes the end.

Britta looks up. Eyes wide.

BRITTA
We won?

OLUWASEUN
You won the end. The match is Thursday.

Britta laughs, helpless. The crowd roars again.

TERRENCE
(to Yuki)
Was that real?

YUKI
(breathless)
I think so.
